<p><strong>Summoned via PM.</strong></p>
<p>Hi there CyborgOctopus. Sorry for the delay. Let’s take a look at “This Channel Does Not Exist”.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">LBL</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">My&nbsp;Mother&nbsp;Doesn’t&nbsp;Exist</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Upon alert, Foundation &quot;Scalpel&quot; software is to remove any media and user accounts created by SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I would specify what kind of alert this is. Like how do we identify these media or user accounts?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In the event that removal is difficult, the affected platform must be disabled until it is complete.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This too is a little vague and unclear: what is “complete”? the process of removal? Disabling it? Understand these are conprocs you were given though, so not much can be done despite simple revisions.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>under the name &quot;This Channel Does Not Exist.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Would specify that it’s under the “account name” as that was your original intention, right?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Videos posted by SCP-XXXX have been designated SCP-XXXX-A. All instances of SCP-XXXX-A</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So this labelling system is a little wonky: if each video is a separate instance, then shouldn’t they be labelled each as SCP-xxxx-1, SCP-xxxx-2 etc.?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>All instances of SCP-XXXX-A had the following characteristics:</p>
</blockquote>
<p>“had” -&gt; “have”.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>obviously artificial props</p>
</blockquote>
<p>May not be “obvious” to regular viewers, so I would specify what makes them “artificial”? Is it because they’re made of cheap materials? Computer CGI?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>fumbled acting</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Hmm. What does this mean? Maybe just “bad” acting?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A performer relevant to the video's subject.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This too is a little unclear, and so is your subsequent explanation. Is it a professional who works in the specified field? An expert in said activity?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This clothing covers the entire head and body, allowing no features to be identified.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So what you mean is a greenscreen suit, right? Why not just specify?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The main notable properties of an SCP-XXXX-A instance</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Cut out either “main” or “notable” – both have similar meaning.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>an SCP-XXXX-A instance</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Because of the complicated labelling system, it isn’t clear what you’re referring to when you mention this phrase.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The main notable properties of an SCP-XXXX-A instance are the erasure of its subject from existence, as well as memory alterations causing everyone aware of the subject to forget most details about it, and disbelieve in its reality.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So this sentence is a little long and clunky. I suggest splitting it up for clarity.</p>
<p>Also, is this line implying that the subject in the videos are <em>real life</em> individuals?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>There is no known upper limit to the scale of these effects.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not really sure what this means. Any number of subjects can disappear or be forgotten?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Attempts to study, interfere, or communicate with SCP-XXXX</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If you define the SCP as the “phenomenon” (as stated earlier), how exactly does the Foundation “communicate” with it. A suggestion is rephrasing it to communicating with the video channel itself through private message or email.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Research should be conducted with extreme caution.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Coincidentally, these research procedures <em>should</em> appear in your conprocs, but I understand that it’s difficult to do so within the contest rules.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Footnote 1</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Would place this right after the name of the protocol itself. Also, the footnote doesn’t provide us with the actual procedure / process of how the protocol is carried out?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Upon further investigation</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, slightly vague, as you’ve left the reader curious as to <em>how</em> research was done into this anomaly and how this information about the researcher was discovered then.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Initial information on SCP-XXXX was found in a Foundation-issued laptop recovered from the missing researcher's home.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is good and intriguing, and the first narrative detail we get. I also wonder if you’d want to hint at Hollister’s role in SCP-xxxx at this point. Maybe hint at what his speciality is that made him involved in one of the videos?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>However, no strong evidence exists of anything out of the ordinary.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This sentence is a little clunky. I’m also not sure what you’re trying to say: “However, no substantial evidence exists of the anomaly”?</p>
<p>On a minor nitpicking point, try paragraphing the emails, no matter how short.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I'm not really supposed to be doing this kind of research from home, but the workload lately has just been so damn big.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Ah? This isn’t really a valid reason to break protocol. Also, didn’t the Site Director already give him permission to work on it?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Now I'm certainly not a linguist, but it sounded kind of…real…I guess</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How can he tell it’s real then?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I decided to play around with the videos in Photoshop a bit. There were some odd things that didn't look like any image compression artifacts I've ever seen. The effects were weird too.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think you have to start being more specific by this point. We already get that the videos are weird and of poor production value – you’ve mentioned that before in the Description. What <em>new</em> information are we getting from these journal entries?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It took me a while, but eventually I started thinking seriously about the channel being anomalous.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Without the specifics, this conclusion <em>doesn’t</em> fly. Thus far, it’s just a weird channel. Why does Hollister conclude that it’s anomalous?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>division manager</p>
</blockquote>
<p>“Division Manager”</p>
<blockquote>
<p>site director</p>
</blockquote>
<p>“Site Director” -&gt; capitalise all titles.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Most Important Points: Unknown language</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This might be the <em>only</em> anomalous property from the video. Then again, it’s not a crime to use a different language in an online video. It might be a little suspicious if the xeno-linguists <em>confirm</em> that the language used was indeed legit and never heard of before, but not before.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Convergence of evidence obtained through linguistic and photographic analysis</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, this isn’t very convincing because you haven’t presented any evidence to the readers yet.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Dr. ████, an on-site linguistics expert, has found parallels between this language and ancient Greek.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Honestly, this entry could have been combined with the previous one. You’re basically saying the same thing in both, just with clearer evidence in the 2nd.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Stopping this now</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So I do like the transition to the final log: he’s slowly losing his faculties and ability to write coherent sentences. What I’m not too sure of are the dreams of the “faceless things”. There’s no prior hints of the SCP being able to affect one’s dreams or memories, so this effect might be viewed as extraneous.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Transcripts with non-English speech have been rendered using the English subtitles provided in the instance.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Note that you tend to repeat information in the addenda titles and then in the foreword of the addenda again. I would revise the foreword to explain the context of each addendum a little more. For example, in Addendum 3, make it clear why the Foundation is studying these videos; why are they important?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Ralph Hollister: Hello. I am here because the producers would like to thank me for appreciating their work. They are great admirers of ours, and wish to collaborate more in the future. I hope there will be no more misunderstandings. [He pauses, his breathing elevated.] I, Ralph Hollister, do not exist.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So this was a nice touch to the ending: actually describing the video that triggered Hollister’s disappearance. What I’m unclear about is what he’s saying, and its implications: Why is SCP-xxxx “great admirers” of the Foundation? And what are its motives such that it wants to “collaborate” and make peace? They <em>did</em> just make one of their researchers disappear, so why is it suddenly peaceful?</p>
<p>Another issue is the timeline of events: does this video appear first or does Hollister disappear first? You might want to indicate the posting date of each of these videos at the start of each transcript, just for clarity.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">clickbait</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>(+/-) I still like the concept, and I think you’ve really expanded on it here in the article, as promised. The ultimate motivations for the virus, however, isn’t made clear by the end, and labelling it as “non-hostile” only makes it more confusing if it’s actively erasing Foundation personnel. I think more can be hinted at what it wants by making all these videos.</p>
<p>One suggestion would be to tie the themes of all these videos together in some way, instead of the current topics which seem haphazard and random. What do Atlantis, Ghosts, Old Ones and Hollister have in common, for example?</p>
<p>(+/-) Structure/Pacing. I think each of the addenda make narrative sense: there’s a clear progression from discovery -&gt; Hollister’s journal entries -&gt; video transcripts. However, I do think there’s a lot of repeated information along the way / deliberately vague details whenever you describe the videos or the reasons for Hollister’s actions, which affect the pacing of the article.</p>
<p>For example, I initially felt the video transcripts in Addenda-3 came too late: they deserved to be front and center to show us what the anomaly actually is. I wonder if you can somehow intersperse those details about what goes on in the video or combine it with Hollister’s research journal about the video.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Overall, at this point, I would no-vote due to the article needing some fine-tuning and extra clarity. You’ve got a lot down of the original concept down on the page, so kudos to you. Best of luck, and if you want me to review the draft again, do drop me another PM!</p>

							</div>

								
					
