			<div class="content" id="post-content-4702922">
									

<p><strong>Resummoned via PM:</strong></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Generally looks cleaner and reader clearer.</p>
<p>A few more concerns I had whilst glancing through a second time:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Its only documented appearance is on the popular global video-sharing website ███████ under the account name &quot;This Channel Does Not Exist.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's a little weird that the platform is blurred out but not the highly specific name of the anomalous account? I don't see why you can't just name the website as &quot;Youtube&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>footnote 1</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's a loooong footnote. Consider, if this much information is critical to our understanding of the anomaly, putting it into the article itself. Otherwise, i would definitely shorten the footnote so that only the most vital info is relayed to us.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>- Addendum 1: Discovery</p>
</blockquote>
<p>use paragraphing to break up important developments in your story. For example, I would begin a new para at &quot;The investigation yielded records of a &quot;Junior Researcher Ralph Hollister.&quot; since this is an entirely new piece of information for the reader.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>██/02/████.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Personally, I wouldn't blur out the Site # or year.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I really wracked my brains, but there was just no easy explanation for this stuff. The videos looked like what you'd get if some alien was trying to imitate human video production techniques with completely different building blocks.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>While this is certainly weird, I still don't think this is considered definitively anomalous. At least not yet. In fact, it still sounds more like pure speculation on the part of the researcher at this point. I know you're trying to describe the video in the weirdest way possible, but I think the most anomalous aspect of the video series right now <strong>only occurs when the reader discovers that your researcher is missing</strong> at the end of the article.</p>
<p>As such, I still have a big issue with the pacing of the article - you're repeating alot of the same information twice. For example, if Hollister already is describing the Atlantis video in Addendum 2, is there a need to show another video transcript of it in Addendum 3?</p>
<p>Consider, for example, if you were to take out everything in Addendum 3 <em>except</em> the last video log titled &quot;Title: Ralph Hollister Does Not Exist&quot;. That serves as the kind of gut punch that brings it home to the reader, who realises that the effect is real.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I'm leaning to an up-vote now but would definitely advise you to seek out other reviewers to also get their two cents worth. All the best!</p>

