<p>oh hey it's later today</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>This feels&#8230;.unsatisfying narratively. Part of the issue is that we have the Items of Interest Recovered from Laptop addendum which summarises everything relatively succinctly but feels more like the intro to a story than the whole story; and then it's followed by the transcripts log which feel&#8230;like they don't really add anything because we already kinda know the gist of what they contain and so the only actually interesting one is the final one.</p>
<p>Even with most of 'em cut, it'd feel unsatisfying because it feels like this lacks closure: there's a lot of mysterious stuff happening but it doesn't feel like it ends up getting resolved, but it also doesn't feel like the mystery is actually that satisfying to think about alone.</p>
<p>It's possible this idea just doesn't really vibe with me tbf; it feels very reminiscent of other kinda media like things like <a href="/scp-2030">SCP-2030</a> but without doing a lot that's unique; but it's possible that other people would find this to be less of an issue.</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Overall</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Line&nbsp;By&nbsp;Line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Line&nbsp;By&nbsp;Line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><em>Gonna note that I can miss stuff; flag things which are right as being wrong and indeed flag things which are deliberately wrong.</em><br />
<em>I'll likely use <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">strikethrough</span> to indicate stuff to remove and <span style="color: green">green text</span> to indicate stuff to add.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>are to be monitored by the Artificially Intelligent Conscript Panoptes.aic for SCP-XXXX</p>
</blockquote>
<p>something about this just being 'for SCP-XXXX' feels iffy, perhaps 'for video media containing SCP-XXXX' would read better?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In the event that the removal procedure is difficult, the affected platform must be disabled until this process is complete.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd probably cut this; 'difficult' is somewhat vague and it feels like it needs to convey something more evocative for it to work here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>are to receive daily memetic screenings. If these reveal any missing memories</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Hm; it feels to me that 'daily memetic screening' and 'checking for missing memories' would be very different kinds of procedure.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The most notable properties of an SCP-XXXX-A instance are the erasure of its subject from existence</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I guess my question here is how do they know that the scp removes the thing from existence rather than contains something which doesn't exist?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The items have been arranged in chronological order to provide a clearer picture of events.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I guess my question is why have all of the dates here been redacted? Why are we allowed to know that there's a Site Director Jackson but not what site he's at? How does the redaction here help protect foundation secrets in universe?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>NOTICE: This email</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd probably trim this footer tbh; it adds an air of realism but it doesn't actually make for interesting reading for the reader.</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;Line&nbsp;By&nbsp;Line</a></div>
</div>
</div>

